    {"name": "route_or_aircraft_type_exposure", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.45}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.3,
  "rationale": "The causal-attribution claim requires BOTH (a) an explicit treatment whose effect on non-recommendation is being attributed, and (b) confounders that are common causes of that treatment and the outcome. Here NO treatment column was added at all -- the original table's service ratings (Seat Comfort, Staff Service, etc.) are the natural candidate causes, but the augmentation did not surface or operationalise any treatment. The two added columns are plausibly pre-treatment covariates surfaced from the data (not copied from the query), which is a point in their favour, but a confounder is only meaningful relative to a treatment; with no treatment defined, they cannot fulfil the confounding role and the causal-estimation setup is incomplete. The columns are more like segmentation/exposure dimensions than a genuine treatment+confounder design, so the technical claim is only weakly satisfied."
